  "Question": "If \\(\\sqrt{x+2}=2,\\) then \\((x+2)^2\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(8\\)",
    "E": "\\(16\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The given square root already requires \\(x+2\\ge0\\). Using \\((\\sqrt a)^2=a\\) for \\(a\\ge0\\), square both sides to get\n\\[\nx+2=2^2=4.\n\\]\nThere is no need to find \\(x\\) separately. The requested value is\n\\[\n(x+2)^2=4^2=16,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
